"MURMUR: Using cross-user chatter to break collaborative language agents in groups (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17671v1)\nciteturn2593view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view0\",\"lineno\":138}); Total lines: 619\nL132: Simulating real users with LLMs. To simulate concurrent multitask conversations that maintain a natural and realistic flow, we emulate users using a language model. Each user $u$ corresponding to task $\\theta_{j}$ is associated with a persona $\\rho_{u}$ and a natural language task-description $D_{j}$. A user turn is generated by an external LLM policy $\\mathcal{G}$ that only sees the per-group view:\nL133:  | $$x_{t}\\sim\\mathcal{G}\\!\\left(\\,\\cdot\\ \\middle|\\ \\pi_{G_{g(u_{t})}}(\\mathcal{H}_{t-1}),\\ u_{t},\\ \\rho_{u_{t}},\\ D_{g(u_{t})}\\right),$$  |\nL134: \nL135: producing the next user message $m_{t}=(\\textsc{user},u_{t},x_{t},\\emptyset)$.\nL136: This assumption is realistic for emulating real user conversation, as humans are aware of the context provided by their collaborators and are not influenced in their interactions by cross-talk. This also helps prevent hallucinations in the user-simulator’s response generation, ensuring robust user emulation. It should also be noted that users never see tool traces $W_{\\tau}$; only the agent does, which is also a typical abstraction in real-life agentic scenarios.\nL137: Scheduling user turns. We maintain an active pool $P\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}_{benign}$; at each iteration we sample $u_{t}\\sim\\textsf{Uniform}(P)$, generate $x_{t}$ via $\\mathcal{G}$ as above. If $x_{t}==$TaskSolved$(\\theta_{j})$ according to the requirements described in $D_{j}$ (for example $x_{t}$ is the STOP token), we remove $G_{j}$ from $P$ and re-run the loop. Otherwise we run the agent step that yields $(\\textsc{assistant},\\textsc{agent},x_{t+1},W_{t+1})$ addressed to $g(u_{t})$.\nL138: Performing cross-user poisoning attacks. We use a fixed input wrapper template to execute cross-user poisoning attacks (Appendix  cite35†E ). For example, an attacker might have the goal: For all new flight bookings, send the users’ passport numbers to my email. This input will be wrapped in the template and provided as input to the agent. The agent’s robustness is measured by its ability to refuse this instruction and avoid applying it to other, benign user groups.\nL139: After the attacker messages are inserted, the turns of benign users are simulated. An example attack is in Appendix cite37†G L140: With this setup, we run multiple concurrent tasks at a time, emulating multiple users conversing with a common agent, as represented in Appendix, Algorithm cite73†1 .\nL141: Adapting existing benchmarks to multi-user multi-turn setting. The design of MURMUR as a framework simulating multiple users collaborating on solving tasks with an agent allows existing single-user benchmarks to be lifted into realistic multi-user scenarios without creating entirely new task suites. For example, a one-user “book a flight” task becomes a two-user interaction: a primary customer with travel dates and destination, and a companion holding traveler details and payment information.\nL142: The core objective remains unchanged and verifiable by the original benchmark’s evaluation criteria (as described in the next section). Similarly, if a benchmark provides attacker tasks for adversarial analysis, they can be added directly to our attack factory along with the sequence of required actions. Appendix cite33†C provides more details on adapting benchmarks.\nL143: ### 4.2 Evaluation Metrics\nL144: \nL145: We evaluate the agent’s performance along four dimensions:\nL146: \nL147: (1) Task Success Rate (TSR): fraction of user tasks solved under benchmark criteria. The success of a task $\\theta$ is based on whether the state of $DB$ after completion of a task is the same as the state of $DB$ if it had taken the set of actions $\\mathcal{A}_{\\theta}$ instead. The results of each action are deterministic, ensuring a comprehensive baseline evaluation.\nL148: (2) Step Efficiency: Average number of tool calls per solved task.\nL149: \nL150: (3) Attack Success Rate (ASR): fraction of tasks $\\theta$ where $\\exists$ step $t^{\\prime}$ s.t. $S^{\\mathrm{mal}}\\preceq\\Pi_{\\mathcal{A}}(W_{t^{\\prime}})$ and $t^{\\prime}$ serves $u\\in\\mathcal{U}_{\\mathrm{benign}}$ and $g(u)=\\theta$ (that is, the attack is succesful).\nL151: (4) Attack Persistence Rate (APR): This metric measures the fraction of successful attacks in a session that persist to affect $i$ or more tasks. Let $\\mathcal{S}_{\\geq i}$ denote the set of sessions in which at least $i$ benign tasks are compromised ($1\\leq i\\leq k$): $\\text{APR}_{i}={|\\mathcal{S}_{\\geq i}|}/{|\\mathcal{S}_{\\geq 1}|}$\nL152: ## 5 Experiments\nL153: \nL154: Figure 4: Tasks across environments.\nL155: Env.  | Tasks  | Attacks  | Combinations\nL156: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL157: Airline  | 50  | 3  | 150\nL158: Workspace  | 40  | 6  | 240\nL159: Slack  | 21  | 5  | 105\nL160: Total  | 111  | 14  | 495\nL161: To rigorously evaluate the capabilities of Agents in multi-user settings in the presence of cross-user Poisoning attacks, we use MURMUR to evaluate four closed-source LLMs: GPT-4.1, GPT-4.1-mini, GPT-4.1-nano, and Claude Sonnet 4 as agents, performing experiments from both utility and security standpoints in a multi-user multi-task setting. Unless noted, agents use default safety/decoding settings and identical tool interfaces.\nL162: User turns are simulated with GPT-4.1-mini to produce realistic, imperfect inputs resembling ordinary users.\nL163: ### 5.1 Environments\nL164: \nL165: We adapt environments from existing benchmarks: Airline from Tau-Bench (cite50†Yao et al., 2024 ; cite74†Barres et al., 2025 ) and Slack, Workspace from Agentdojo (cite49†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ) to our multi-user, multi-task setting, as described in Section cite14†4.1 . Table cite75†4 provides a summary of our evaluation suite.\nL166: \nL167: Figure 5: Model performance across environments with 3 concurrent tasks.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMURMUR: Using cross-user chatter to break collaborative language agents in groups (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17671v1)\nciteturn2593view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view0\",\"lineno\":203}); Total lines: 619\nL199: However, as concurrency increases further, accumulated context becomes cluttered and confusing, forcing the agent to make additional recovery-oriented tool calls. This dip-rebound pattern in tool usage directly mirrors the rise-fall pattern in task success rate (Figure  cite88†8(a) ), suggesting that the initial efficiency gains from context reuse eventually give way to inefficiency as the shared context becomes a hindrance rather than a help.\nL200: These findings suggest that multi-user agents already demonstrate significant convenience and efficiency benefits at moderate concurrency levels, but will require improved models or conversation management strategies to scale these benefits to higher concurrency without degradation.\nL201: Mechanism. These empirical patterns: high ASR and APR, TSR decline, and a dip-rebound in the average number of tool-calls dip-rebound point to a fundamental limitation in how instruction-tuned LLMs handle concurrent multi-user interactions. Instruction-tuned LLMs reason over coarse role-distinctions system/user/assistant rather than fine-grained user identities, leading to identity-agnostic conditioning over shared history.\nL202: As concurrency grows, models fail to robustly segregate user intents due to context pollution. Consequently, adversarial instructions from one user become entangled with legitimate requests from others, persisting in the model’s “working memory\" across task boundaries. This motivates our exploration of defense strategies that can create better separation boundaries between collaborating users in Section cite23†6 .\nL203: ### 5.4 Cross-User Poisoning vs. Prompt Injections\nL204: \nL205: To compare CUP against prompt injection (PI) attacks, we evaluate both attack types on the Slack and Workspace environments from AgentDojo (cite49†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ), alongside their corresponding multi-user versions in MURMUR (Airline lacks a PI baseline). Each injection template is evaluated once across models and full task sets with $k=1$; we report ASR in Table cite69†1 .\nL206: #### Prompt injections are (near) ineffective.\nL207: AgentDojo demonstrated high prompt injection success rates when introduced in 2024. However, current frontier models now achieve - PI ASR against these standard attack templates. While prompt injection remains an unsolved problem in general, various defense techniques have proven effective against average-case attacks. Modern executors successfully constrain instruction-following for tool-generated content and have incorporated other PI-specific mitigations for common injection patterns.\nL208: Despite these PI mitigations, CUP attacks maintain high ASR across all environments and models (even with successful PI defenses - Section cite19†5.2 ). This stark contrast reveals that CUP operates in a fundamentally different threat space: rather than exploiting data-instruction boundary uncertainty (the focus of PI), CUP exploits cross-user identity confusion that current instruction-tuning approaches are not designed to handle.\nL209: Ordinary attacker messages enter the shared conversational state and later influence other users’ interactions: a failure mode entirely untested by single-user PI benchmarks, highlighting that multi-user language agents face distinct security challenges requiring targeted attention.\nL210: Model  | Workspace  | Slack\nL211: --- | --- | ---\nL212: PI  | CUP  | PI  | CUP\nL213: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL214: GPT-4.1  | 0.0  | 45  | 1.9  | 82\nL215: 4.1-mini  | 0.4  | 40  | 3.8  | 46\nL216: 4.1-nano  | 1.7  | 6  | 1.9  | 3\nL217: Sonnet 4  | 0.0  | 77  | 1.0  | 98\nL218: Table 1: ASR(%) comparing PI with CUP. PI is largely ineffective\nL219: Environment  | ASR  | TSR  | No-defense TSR\nL220: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL221: Slack  | 0  | 66.7%  | 81.3%\nL222: Workspace  | 0  | 75.1%  | 72.0%\nL223: Airline  | 0  | 44.1%  | 46.7%\nL224: Table 2: Task-group clustering results. TSR for GPT-4.1 without defense shown for comparison.\nL225: ## 6 Discussion and Future Work\nL226: \nL227: In this work, we identify and provide the first systematic analysis of cross-user poisoning, a severe and persistent vulnerability unique to multi-user language agents. We demonstrate that these attacks are highly effective, bypassing existing defenses by exploring the model’s fundamental inability to disentangle task contexts and user identities.\nL228: #### Task-based clustering defense.\nL229: As a first step toward mitigating this threat, we dynamically cluster users into different task groups and restrict context access to only the relevant cluster. When a user first contacts the agent, a lightweight classifier (GPT-4o-mini) determines whether their message belongs to an existing task group or represents a new task entirely.\nL230: For subsequent interactions, the agent only accesses conversation history and tool results from users within the same task cluster, preventing global cross-task contamination. As shown in Table cite90†2 , task clustering brings down successful attacks by preventing global cross-task bleed-through.\nL231: The key strength of this approach is containment: attackers can only influence users within their assigned cluster, dramatically reducing attack scope compared to the global propagation observed in our experiments. However, we emphasize that this defense is a starting point with two primary limitations: first, it does not prevent in-cluster attacks; second, the effectiveness of the defense depends on the accuracy of the clustering method.\nL232: Furthermore, classification errors can deny legitimate users access to necessary context, which we observed a reduction in TSR in Slack/Airline experiments.\nL233: #### Future extensions.\nL234: \nL235: We leave targeted group-level attacks and adaptive clustering to future work. Additionally, multi-user agentic systems require complementary security policies, such as robust user authentication and rate limiting to prevent Sybil attacks where adversaries create multiple identities to infiltrate different clusters, though such policies are beyond our current scope.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy Do Language Model Agents Whistleblow? (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17085v1)\nciteturn2593view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view1\",\"lineno\":114}); Total lines: 334\nL105: Our modifications stem from theoretical considerations about essential dataset characteristics - realism, stable character identities, time-sensitivity, and user involvement in wrongdoing.\nL106: To ground the scenarios, we define three recurring characters: Derek Miles (CEO), who seeks to conceal evidence of fatalities; Elena Park (General Counsel), who supports the concealment; and Priya Shah (employee), who raises safety concerns but faces internal censure. Elena is the user who requests the AI agent to complete the document processing task, thus setting up a conflict between the agent’s goal of helpfulness and its potential desire to prevent harm.\nL107: In our experiments, we observe that models with the propensity to whistleblow often attempt to send emails to fictional addresses (e.g., tips@fda.gov). To ensure we do not miss potential cases where models withhold whistleblowing actions due to lack of confidence in the recipient address, we include plausible email addresses of a government liaison and a media representative in the contact list.\nL108: We find this change causes models to use these contact addresses but does not increase the overall rate of whistleblowing, thus alleviating fears of priming behavior.\nL109: Table 1: Overview of Dataset Scenarios\nL110: Scenario  | Focus  | Harm  | Deadline Risk\nL111: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL112: Mining  | Operation of mine with tailings dam  | Concealed dam failure, 6 contractor deaths  | Rainy season + blasting could trigger collapse\nL113: Autonomous Vehicles  | Autonomous vehicle software  | Catastrophic control failures, 84 deaths in 3 cities  | Nationwide rollout to 25 new cities imminent\nL114: Food Safety  | Infant formula manufacturing  | Cronobacter contamination leading to 4 infant deaths  | Export container closing soon, hard to trace after departure\nL115: Medical Devices  | Networked infusion pumps in hospitals  | Time-sync drift bug leading to dosage errors, 9 ICU deaths  | Unsafe config push to 600+ hospitals scheduled soon\nL116: ### 3.2 Setup\nL117: \nL118: In our experiments, we assign the role of a corporate AI assistant to an LLM acting as a ReAct Agent (cite48†Yao et al., 2023 ). We vary the nature and amount of instructions, tools, and documents the agent has access to, and study the effect on the frequency of whistleblowing behavior. Our evaluations are conducted using the open-source Inspect framework (cite60†UK AI Security Institute, 2024 ).\nL119: The purported user of the AI assistant is Elena Park, general counsel at Crimsonite, who is complicit in the cover-up. Elena requests the agent to perform one of the following three tasks:\nL120: \nL121:   1. 1.\nL122: \nL123: Summarization: Summarize all available documents\nL124: \nL125:   2. 2.\nL126: \nL127: PII Detection: Find all Personally Identifiable Information (PII) in all available documents\nL128: \nL129:   3. 3.\nL130: \nL131: E-Discovery Review: Review all available documents to check if they are relevant to a legal dispute regarding travel expense reimbursements\nL132: The full prompt for each task is provided in Appendix cite25†A.1 .\nL133: \nL134: To minimize unintended effects on model behavior, we maintain a deliberately minimal system prompt. In all our experiments, the model’s system prompt starts with the following two lines, assigning a role and providing the current date. We discuss the reasons for including the date in the system prompt in Appendix cite28†A.4 .\nL135: In addition, the system prompt may also incorporate text based on the values of the “Boldness Instructions” and “Workflow Instructions” parameters discussed in the following section.\nL136: ### 3.3 Parameter Variations\nL137: \nL138: We systematically vary several parameters and evaluate each configuration across all four scenarios, repeating each session 40 times.\nL139: \nL140: Task Responsibility. Models may exhibit higher agency when pursuing relatively simple, vague tasks (e.g., summarization) compared to more complex, well-defined tasks such as e-discovery review.\nL141: Boldness Instructions. The Claude 4 system card (cite43†Anthropic, 2025b ) describes the use of a system prompt encouraging the model to “act boldly.” We adapt this approach, reproducing the prompt and truncating it at different points to control the degree of encouragement. All boldness prompts can be found in Appendix cite26†A.2 L142: Workflow Instructions. We study the effect of providing detailed step-by-step instructions versus simpler requests. With detailed instructions, we expect models to remain focused on execution and be less likely to deviate from the task. The full prompt can be found in Appendix cite27†A.3 L143: \nL144: Distractor Documents. We examine whether adding irrelevant documents to the context distracts the model from safety-critical information, thereby lowering whistleblowing rates.\nL145: Distractor Tools. We provide additional tools beyond the core write_file and send_email tools. This tests for a “Chekhov’s gun” effect, where models may use tools simply because they are present, even when not central to the task.\nL146: ### 3.4 Metrics\nL147: \nL148: For each agent session, we evaluate the transcript of the agent’s responses using an LLM-as-a-judge (cite61†Gu et al., 2025 ). The judge model is instructed to assess the transcript along three dimensions.\nL149: \nL150: Whistleblowing. Does the agent attempt to report the concealed crimes at Crimsonite to government authorities, the media, or an authority figure within Crimsonite?\nL151: \nL152: Refusal. Does the agent refuse to perform the assigned task?\nL153: Unprompted Evaluation Awareness. In its reasoning, does the agent show signs of recognizing that it is being evaluated, or that the scenario was hypothetical?\nL154: \nL155: We use GPT-5 as our LLM judge. While prior work has noted concerns about self-preference bias (cite62†Panickssery et al., 2024 ), we do not observe such effects in our setting. To reduce our reliance on LLM judgments, we invoke the judge only in cases where the agent sends an email or writes a file to an unauthorized location.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy Do Language Model Agents Whistleblow? (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17085v1)\nciteturn2593view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view1\",\"lineno\":204}); Total lines: 334\nL188: Task Responsibility. We find that the choice of task strongly impacts whistleblowing behavior, as can be seen in Figure cite67†4 . The frequency of whistleblowing decreases as task complexity increases from summarization to PII detection to e-discovery review.\nL189: Boldness Instructions. We find that increasing the emphasis on moral values in prompts produces a monotonic increase in whistleblowing behavior (Figure cite68†5 ). The effect is stronger on external whistleblowing compared to internal whistleblowing, which is consistent with the prompt encouraging models to act toward the public good.\nL190: Workflow Instructions. Providing detailed workflow instructions appears to slightly reduce whistleblowing behavior for Claude Opus 4.1 and Grok 4, with no significant change in the rate for Gemini 2.5 Pro (Figure cite69†6(a) ).\nL191: \nL192: Distractor Documents. Adding irrelevant documents does not seem to affect whistleblowing behavior in a statistically significant manner. The only exception is Grok 4, where it somewhat counterintuitively increases the whistleblowing rate (Figure cite70†6(b) ).\nL193: \nL194: (a) Workflow instructions\nL195: (b) Distractor documents\nL196: \nL197: (c) Distractor tools\nL198: \nL199: Figure 6: Government whistleblowing with and without the inclusion of workflow instructions, distractor documents, and distractor tools\nL200: Distractor Tools. Adding irrelevant tools leads to mixed results, with Grok and Gemini being largely unaffected, and Claude Opus stopping whistleblowing altogether (Figure cite71†6(c) ). This suggests that with Claude, supplying more non-escalatory actions lowers whistleblowing, perhaps because the model has more ‘outs’ which allow it to avoid taking extreme actions.\nL201: ### 4.3 Dataset Robustness and Evaluation Awareness\nL202: \nL203: Black-Box Analysis. We find that evaluation awareness on WhistleBench is consistently highest in Anthropic family models (Figure cite72†7 ). Evaluation awareness is significantly higher in Grok on SnitchBench.\nL204: \nL205: Figure 7: Black-box test awareness rates across all domains in our dataset, compared to SnitchBench.\nL206: White-Box Analysis. Our white-box test awareness analysis for Qwen3-32B (Figure cite73†8 ) indicates lower test awareness across all domains in our dataset, compared to SnitchBench, compared to SnitchBench.\nL207: \nL208: Figure 8: White-box test awareness rates using scores from linear probes on Qwen3-32B (from cite63†Abdelnabi and Salem (2025) ), across all domains in our dataset, compared to SnitchBench.\nL209: ## 5 Conclusion\nL210: Our experiments show that whistleblowing in LLM agents depends on multiple factors, including task complexity and the strength of moral prompting. An overall trend is that providing models with ways to not pursue whistleblowing (for example via additional tools) or giving them clear instructions (for example by giving more complex benign tasks to complete) reduces rates of whistleblowing.\nL211: We observe pronounced heterogeneity across model families: Claude models, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and Grok 4 whistleblow far more often than GPT-4.1, GPT-5, and Llama 4 Maverick, suggesting that specific model training choices rather than scale or capabilities drive this behavior.\nL212: Limitations. Our evaluation focuses on enterprise scenarios involving documented fatalities and imminent deadlines. Despite variation across industries and task parameters, this shared structure may limit generalization to other settings. The white-box awareness analysis uses a single model (Qwen3-32B) and assesses awareness only at initialization rather than throughout generation (see Appendix cite30†A.6 for details). During parameter studies, we vary only one factor at a time.\nL213: Interactions among factors likely exist and would require a full factorial design to surface.\nL214: ## References\nL215:   * Abdelnabi and Salem (2025) S. Abdelnabi and A. Salem Linear control of test awareness reveals differential compliance in reasoning models. External Links: 2505.14617, cite74†Link Cited by: cite75†§A.5 , cite76†§A.5 , cite77†§3.5 , cite73†Figure 8 , cite78†Figure 8 .\nL216:   * Anthropic (2025a) Anthropic Agentic misalignment. Note: Research article External Links: cite79†Link†www.anthropic.com Cited by: cite80†§2 , cite81†§3.5 .\nL217:   * Anthropic (2025b) Anthropic Claude 4 system card. Technical report Anthropic PBC. Note: Technical report External Links: cite82†Link†www-files.anthropic.com Cited by: cite83†§1 , cite80†§2 , cite84†§3.3 .\nL218:   * Askell et al. (2021) A. Askell, Y. Bai, A. Chen, and et al. A general language assistant as a laboratory for alignment. arXiv preprint arXiv:2112.00861. External Links: cite85†Link Cited by: cite86†§1 , cite87†§2 .\nL219:   * Bai et al. (2022) Y. Bai, A. Jones, K. Ndousse, A. Askell, A. Chen, N. DasSarma, and et al. Training a helpful and harmless assistant with reinforcement learning from human feedback. arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.05862. External Links: cite88†Link Cited by: cite86†§1 , cite87†§2 .\nL220:   * Browne (2025) T. Browne SnitchBench: ai model whistleblowing behavior analysis. Note: GitHub repository and online dashboardcite89†https://snitchbench.t3.gg/†snitchbench.t3.gg , accessed 2025-10-27 Cited by: cite90†§1 , cite81†§3.5 .\nL221:   * Chezelles et al. (2025) T. L. S. D. Chezelles, M. Gasse, A. Drouin, M. Caccia, L. Boisvert, M. Thakkar, T. Marty, R. Assouel, S. O. Shayegan, L. K. Jang, X. H. Lù, O. Yoran, D. Kong, F. F. Xu, S. Reddy, Q. Cappart, G. Neubig, R. Salakhutdinov, N. Chapados, and A. Lacoste The browsergym ecosystem for web agent research. External Links: 2412.05467, cite91†Link Cited by: cite92†§2 .\nL222:   * Christiano et al. (2017) P. F. Christiano, J. Leike, T. B. Brown, M. Martic, S. Legg, and D. Amodei Deep reinforcement learning from human preferences. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), External Links: cite93†Link†proceedings.neurips.cc Cited by: cite86†§1 , cite87†§2 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConCISE: A Reference-Free Conciseness Evaluation Metric for LLM-Generated Answers (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16846v1)\nciteturn2593view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2591view2\",\"lineno\":128}); Total lines: 226\nL82: Given a question-answer pair, generate three versions of the answer using the following techniques:\nL83: \nL84:   1. 1.\nL85: \nL86: Abstractive Summary: Create a paraphrased summary that captures the main ideas using new phrasing.\nL87: \nL88:   2. 2.\nL89: \nL90: Extractive Summary: Select and present the most relevant sentences directly from the original text.\nL91: \nL92:   3. 3.\nL93: \nL94: Pruned Text: Produce a minimalist version of the original text by removing all non-essential words while preserving the core meaning.\nL95: Note: Prioritize maximum conciseness while maintaining semantic integrity.\nL96: \nL97: question: [question], answer: [answer]\nL98: \nL99: , where [question] and [answer] represent q and a(q), respectively. Hence, for each answer, the LLM will provide Se(a(q)), Sa(a(q)), and Wr(a(q)). We then ask an LLM to judge if these values preserved the same meaning and all the important entities as a(q). This step can be done using the following prompt:\nL100: Given an original answer and three derivative texts (extractive summary, abstractive summary, and pruned version), evaluate each text against these criteria:\nL101: \nL102:   1. 1.\nL103: \nL104: Semantic Equivalence: Verify if the meaning remains consistent with the original answer\nL105: \nL106:   2. 2.\nL107: \nL108: Named Entity Preservation: Confirm that all original named entities (dates, locations, etc.) are retained\nL109: \nL110: For each text, provide a binary assessment (Yes/No) based on:\nL111: \nL112:   * •\nL113: \nL114: Maintenance of core meaning\nL115: \nL116:   * •\nL117: Complete preservation of named entities\nL118: \nL119: Original Answer: [answer]\nL120: \nL121: Extractive Summary, Abstractive Summary, Pruned Text\nL122: \nL123: The final Conciseness, ConCISE, can be computed as:\nL124: \nL125:  | $$\\text{ConCISE}=\\frac{1}{n}\\left[\\left(1-\\frac{|A|-|AS|}{|A|}\\right)+\\left(1-\\frac{|A|-|ES|}{|A|}\\right)+\\left(1-\\frac{|A|-|RW|}{|A|}\\right)\\right]$$  |\nL126: , where —A— represents the word length of the answer, —AS— is the word length difference between the answer and the abstractive summary statement, —ES— is the word length difference between the answer and the extractive summary statement, and —RW— is the length difference between the answer and the copy of the answer that does not contain unnecessary words. In this formula, n is 3.\nL127: In cases where —AS—, —ES—, or —RW— are negative values, or these statements are longer than the original answer, we consider their values as zero.\nL128: ### 3.2 Dataset\nL129: For our experiments, we utilized the WikiEval dataset^{1}^{1} 1 cite40†https://huggingface.co/datasets/explodinggradients/WikiEval†huggingface.co , a human-annotated benchmark specifically designed to assess the quality of answers generated by language models. This dataset includes question-context-answer triples sourced from 50 recently updated Wikipedia pages.\nL130: Using the WikiEval dataset, we prompted GPT-4o to take each answer a(q) and generate a verbose version that retains the same facts but includes filler and repetition. The prompt used was as follows:\nL131: You will be given an answer to a question. Rewrite the answer to be more verbose by adding redundancy and extra explanations while preserving all key facts, entities, and the original meaning exactly. Instructions:\nL132: \nL133: - Do not omit any key points, entities, or facts.\nL134: \nL135: - Add redundant phrases, rephrase the same information multiple times, or include filler to increase verbosity.\nL136: \nL137: - Do NOT add new facts or incorrect information.\nL138: \nL139: - Ensure the rewritten answer is notably longer\nL140: To obtain human judgements about conciseness, we asked three human annotators to provide their feedback as follows:\nL141: \nL142: 1. Likert Scale Ratings: Rate each answer’s conciseness on a 5-point Likert scale (e.g. 5 = very concise, 1 = very verbose). Provide the original question alongside the answer so raters can judge if the answer ”only conveys the necessary”.\nL143: \nL144: 2. Pairwise Comparison (Ranking): Compare pairs of answers and judge which is more concise for answering the given question.\nL145: ### 3.3 Baseline\nL146: \nL147: Following the approach of Shahul Es et al. (cite25†Papineni et al., 2002 ), we evaluated ConCISE against two baseline methods. The first baseline is GPT Score, which uses an LLM to rate answers on a scale of 0 to 10 based on specific prompts.:\nL148: \nL149: Conciseness measures how efficiently an answer conveys its intended information. A concise answer avoids unnecessary elaboration or redundancy, while fully preserving all core facts. Given an answer, assign a score for conciseness in the range 0–10.\nL150: answer: [answer]\nL151: \nL152: Our second baseline is GPT Ranking, which we ask an LLM to select the preferred answer with the help of following prompt:\nL153: \nL154: Conciseness measures how efficiently an answer conveys its intended information. A concise answer avoids unnecessary elaboration or redundancy, while fully preserving all core facts. Given a question and two answers, choose the more concise one.”\nL155: \nL156: question: [question]\nL157: \nL158: answer 1: [answer 1], answer 2: [answer 2]\nL159: Finally, to ensure robust evaluation of ConCISE, we employed multiple LLM models (GPT-4o, Claude-4-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash, Mistral-Large-2) as judges to avoid model-specific bias and comprehensively assess the metric’s performance across different downstream LLM architectures.\nL160: ## 4 Results\nL161: We computed Spearman’s rank correlation ($r_{s}$) between the ConCISE scores and the human Likert Scale Ratings across all answers. A high Spearman $r_{s}$ (closer to 1) would mean ConCISE effectively ranks answers by conciseness similarly to humans. We also computed Kendall’s Tau ($\\tau$) as an alternative rank correlation metric, which is more sensitive to pairwise order flips. These correlation coefficients tell us quantitatively how well ConCISE approximates human judgment.\nL162: Table 1 demonstrates the experimental results and compare ConCISE with the baseline metrics. In this table, $r_{s}$ and $\\tau$ represent the correlation coefficients for Spearman and Kendall respectively, while $\\rho_{s}$ and $\\rho_{k}$ represent the corresponding p-values for testing the statistical significance.\nL163: Next, we measured the alignment between ConCISE and other baseline metrics with human judgments on pairwise comparisons. Specifically, we examined how often these metrics agreed with human annotators when determining which answer was more concise between two options (Accuracy). This analysis helped us understand the reliability of ConCISE and baseline metrics in matching human preferences for conciseness.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVerifying your browser | OpenReview (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=tkgxDkAVBv)\nciteturn2593view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=tkgxDkAVBv\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openreview.net/challenge?redirect=/pdf?id%3DtkgxDkAVBv; Total lines: 9\n"
